WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.330
It reminds me of other...
Other expressions, other words.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:08.200
Hello, this is Steve Kaufman

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.777
and I want to talk about, 
of course, language learning

00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:15.300
but above all about the
importance of forgetting...

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.190
when learning words
and languages.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:25.430
If you like my videos,
please subscribe.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.070
Click on the bell
to get notifications.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.900
And if you’re following me 
through a Podcast service

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.320
Please leave a comment.
I really appreciate it.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.267
Good.

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:42.460
I often hear people saying
"I have a bad memory"

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:45.120
"I have difficulty in
learning languages"

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.100
"I don't have the talent
to learn languages"

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.370
And I always say that
learning languages

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.925
is a question of 
acquiring habits

00:00:53.925 --> 00:00:56.860
and it is not a
question of talent...

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.226
There may be
talent differences

00:00:59.226 --> 00:01:01.972
but that's not the
most important thing

00:01:01.972 --> 00:01:06.756
but it is about wanting to
get exposed to the language

00:01:06.756 --> 00:01:11.540
and not worrying too much
about things we forget.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:18.850
And that indeed, forgetting is a
very important part of learning

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.895
and because...
Inevitably, we will forget

00:01:21.895 --> 00:01:23.841
everything we learn,
we will forget it

00:01:23.841 --> 00:01:28.740
and we will relearn it and
then, again, forget it.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.225
And there is researches that
were made in California

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:38.570
A professor, Robert Bjork,
who demonstrates that...

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:44.080
Our memory is
not only in things

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:51.120
that we can remember, that
we can fetch in a context

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.330
when we are talking,
when we read something.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:02:00.875
But memory also consists
in what he calls the "Reserve"

00:02:00.875 --> 00:02:07.347
that is to say things that we’ve knew
or that we saw and that we heared

00:02:07.347 --> 00:02:10.680
and who entered our memory

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:17.760
but that we are not 
able to find or look for.

00:02:17.761 --> 00:02:20.580
"to retrieve them"
as we say in English.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:25.020
But these are still things
which are part of our memory.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:29.130
And in language learning,
these are the things we forget.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.779
And often, as Proust
said, things that we...

00:02:31.779 --> 00:02:34.100
Maybe sometimes 
unpleasant things or things…

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:39.720
where there is no effort
purposely to remember them.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:46.850
These are the things we're going
to remember at some point.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.947
And even me,
I had this experience

00:02:48.947 --> 00:02:53.027
because I am trying
to prepare a speech

00:02:53.027 --> 00:02:57.180
that I will give to
lawyers going to Ukraine.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.250
And I will give
this presentation

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.590
on language learning and it is
actually in Ukrainian and English.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:07.499
So I decided to refresh my 
Ukrainian language a little

00:03:07.499 --> 00:03:13.260
and so I studied the mini stories
we have at LingQ and by doing so...

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:21.210
I saw that even by relearning fairly
common high frequency words

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:26.260
it reminds me of other
expressions and other words.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:31.740
So it stimulated me to dig
in what was in memory

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:38.250
let's say directly there, 
words that I was relearning.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.300
There are other words
that I wasn’t reading

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.320
that I wasn't hearing, that I wasn't
listening to and who reappeared

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:49.360
So, that is to say,
the reserve that we create

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.280
every time we listen and read

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.860
even if we forget the word,
we forget the expression.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.706
These are things that
are saved in our memory

00:03:58.706 --> 00:04:06.000
and it is by forgetting the words and the
expressions, that we increase the reserve.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.680
And it's from these 
words there, possibly

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:15.180
that we’ll finally create an important 
vocabulary that will allow us

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:22.540
to have interesting 
conversations with people

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.082
understand books, etc.

00:04:26.082 --> 00:04:29.310
and in the LingQ 
system in particular.

00:04:30.172 --> 00:04:35.850
I realized that since the first
time we encounter a word in LingQ

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:41.000
it is underlined in blue and
then we convert it to LingQ.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.440
So it gets a yellow color,
but all the words in yellow...

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.070
These are words that 
we can't remember.

00:04:48.070 --> 00:04:54.520
Because the words we 
understand in any context.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.330
These are known words.

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:58.660
So if I look at my stats

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.590
in different languages
that I studied on LingQ

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:02.910
Well...

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:09.081
For example Ukrainian,
I know about 60,000 words

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:14.360
and actually fewer yellow 
words, that is to say words

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:18.688
which are part of my reserve
because it's a language...

00:05:18.688 --> 00:05:25.540
I listened to and read a lot and
even studying Russian or Czech

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:31.050
helped me a lot to recognize
words when studying Ukrainian.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:39.240
On the other hand, if I look at my
statistics in Arabic or Persian.

00:05:39.733 --> 00:05:44.956
Well, there are three times 
more words in my reserve

00:05:44.956 --> 00:05:47.508
that is to say words that
I have met, that I have read

00:05:47.508 --> 00:05:51.040
that I looked for 
and then I forgot.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.137
It's three times more
than the words that I knew

00:05:56.137 --> 00:05:58.830
that I can recognize
in a context.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:04.040
In the case of Arabic, I have about
60,000 or even more words

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:06.610
that I went looking for the
meaning in the dictionary.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:11.140
I put them in my database.
It's part of my reserve

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:16.394
but I can't recognize only 20,000
of these words, so just a third.

00:06:16.394 --> 00:06:18.540
The case of Persian is similar.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:21.770
I have about 30,000
words that I’ve saved

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:26.870
which are in my database and
are therefore part of my reserve.

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:31.530
But I only have 
12,000 known words

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.293
that is to say that each time
I see them, I understand them

00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:37.200
and that's totally
fine, that's normal.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:39.180
And finally, if I keep going

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.690
in the end, I would have 
more words that I can use.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:49.750
But the important thing here is
that one shouldn’t demand too much

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:53.630
that we can immediately recall
words that we have studied.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:57.473
We must accept forgetting,
“creative forgetting”

00:06:57.803 --> 00:07:02.140
The fact that all these
listening and reading activities

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:06.480
and seeing words, listening
to words and expressions

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.512
grow our, let's say...

00:07:09.512 --> 00:07:13.610
facilitate our language habits
with this new language.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:16.770
And make also 
our reserve grow.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:20.150
So even if we have the
impression of not progressing

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:25.233
because we are no longer
in this beginners course

00:07:25.233 --> 00:07:30.360
where there are so many frequent 
words that are learned fairly quickly.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.460
Now we're in this
called “Plateau”.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:37.110
So there, you have to realize
that by reading and listening

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:42.140
by increasing our reserve,
we create the conditions.

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:45.990
So, I mean, by forgetting
words, we create the conditions

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:52.272
that will allow us to reach a 
level, let’s say, in the language

00:07:52.272 --> 00:07:56.790
the desired level of ability
to speak the language fluently.

00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:59.632
Well, that's what I wanted
to say about this subject.

00:07:59.632 --> 00:08:04.974
and I’ll leave some videos I have
done about this memory subject.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:08.120
Concerning languages learning.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.690
Thanks! Goodbye.

